00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.030
interesting things, I think.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.530
So we went with a
static site generator

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:24.200
for the website, a
project called Hugo.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:25.700
We did that for a
number of reasons,

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:27.300
mainly because
it's written in Go,

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.040
and Francesc likes Go a lot.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:30.706
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yay.

00:08:30.706 --> 00:08:33.039
MARK MANDEL: So it is actually
a really nice static site

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:34.600
generator.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:36.539
So we built the
website with that.

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:39.650
And we could extend it to do
all this stuff for podcasting,

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:42.000
and it worked
really, really well.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.140
So once we had all those static
files that Hugo generates,

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:47.820
we needed somewhere
to host that.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:48.930
So, that makes sense.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:52.250
We ended-- we actually had
a few options with that.

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:55.107
We ended up actually
going with hosting stuff--

00:08:55.107 --> 00:08:56.690
hosting our static
files on App Engine

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.900
itself, which is a little bit
maybe controversial, which

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:00.560
is interesting.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:03.570
But we did it for, I think, a
few good reasons, one of which

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:07.334
is App Engine gives us really
easy access to HTTPS support.

00:09:07.334 --> 00:09:08.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:10.791
MARK MANDEL: So you can bundle
it up, stick it on App Engine,

00:09:10.791 --> 00:09:12.597
put it up in the
cloud, magic cloud.

00:09:12.597 --> 00:09:15.055
It still has edge caching, much
like Google Cloud Storage--

00:09:15.055 --> 00:09:15.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, true.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:17.269
MARK MANDEL: --which
is really nice.

00:09:17.269 --> 00:09:19.020
We can also upload
multiple versions

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.314
that can be served at the
same time on different URLs.

00:09:21.314 --> 00:09:22.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:24.480
That's something that I
really like, the fact that you

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.130
can do-- not beta testing.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:26.929
I forgot.

00:09:26.929 --> 00:09:27.970
MARK MANDEL: A/B testing.

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:29.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: A/B
testing, that's it.

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:30.960
So yeah, you can do A/B testing.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:33.865
And see what [INAUDIBLE]
version is more performant,

00:09:33.865 --> 00:09:34.680
things like that.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:35.110
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:35.943
And it's great, too.

00:09:35.943 --> 00:09:38.250
Like I can make a
podcast episode,

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:40.680
put it up on a different URL,
send it to Francesc and say,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.060
hey, does this
look right to you?

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:42.992
He's like yeah, sweet.

00:09:42.992 --> 00:09:44.450
I hit a button in
the dashboard, it

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.090
switches it out
with no downtime,

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:46.860
which is really nice, too.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:47.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's very nice.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:48.200
Yeah.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:50.870
MARK MANDEL: And the other
reason we went with App Engine

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:54.169
is because we can also
do dynamic content.

00:09:54.169 --> 00:09:56.210
So maybe I should actually
step back a little bit

00:09:56.210 --> 00:09:57.960
and talk a little bit
about what App Engine is,

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.410
for those people who don't know.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:01.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That sounds
like a very good idea indeed.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:02.186
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:10:02.186 --> 00:10:04.314
So App Engine is
one of our-- well,

00:10:04.314 --> 00:10:07.430
is our primary Platform
as a Service offerings.

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:10.810
So what that really means
is we kind of say, hey,

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:13.394
you need to write
your code this way.

00:10:13.394 --> 00:10:15.060
We're going to kind
of dictate it to you

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:17.610
and, as long as you're happy
to do that, then that means

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:19.452
we can do stuff like
really fast scaling,

00:10:19.452 --> 00:10:21.660
we can provide you with a
whole bunch of services out

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:24.750
of the box, deployment
is really easy,

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:26.280
because we're able
to sort of adjust

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:28.230
based on those constraints
we put upon you.

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:30.335
And yeah, as long as that fits
with you, it's pretty cool.

00:10:30.335 --> 00:10:31.800
So I mean, there's
a lot of customers

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.810
we have that use App
Engine and like the model,

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:35.907
because they can just be
like, oh, I don't really

00:10:35.907 --> 00:10:37.990
care how much traffic I
get, because App Engine is

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:40.680
going to handle it for
me, and that's awesome.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:41.870
So it's pretty cool.

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:45.170
I mean, it supports Go,
it supports PHP, Java,

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:45.880
and Python--

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:46.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:47.600
MARK MANDEL: --out of the box.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:49.920
And so those are some
pretty decent languages

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:51.529
that have some
pretty decent, like,

00:10:51.529 --> 00:10:52.820
penetration into the community.

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:52.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.245
The thing that I really
like about App Engine is

00:10:55.245 --> 00:10:58.370
that basically what you do is
you just give us your code.

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:01.170
And there's some restrictions on
what kind of code you can run,

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:03.740
but you basically
just give us your code

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.580
and, if it matches
our restrictions,

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.497
then we just run it for
you, whatever happens.

00:11:07.497 --> 00:11:08.871
MARK MANDEL: And
the restrictions

00:11:08.871 --> 00:11:10.290
are pretty, like, reasonable.

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:11.970
I mean, it's for
web stuff, right?

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.712
So it's no requests
over 60 seconds.

00:11:13.712 --> 00:11:15.795
Well, you probably shouldn't
be doing that anyway.

00:11:15.795 --> 00:11:16.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.390
And there's things like
you cannot run C++,

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:19.220
things like that.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:20.720
But if you want to do
that, we have other--

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:21.800
MARK MANDEL: We have other
stuff for that sort of thing.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:22.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, we
have other places to run it.

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:26.010
And yeah, there's-- some of
the customers that run this,

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.510
it's actually pretty impressive,
because you think like, oh,

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:30.301
yeah, it's something
that's going to scale.

00:11:30.301 --> 00:11:31.580
But can I actually trust it?

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:32.330
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:34.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There's
Rovio that uses it.

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:35.950
There's Khan Academy
that uses it.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:36.280
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:37.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
There's Snapchat that--

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:37.600
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:38.309
Snapchat is huge.

00:11:38.309 --> 00:11:39.225
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:11:39.225 --> 00:11:39.810
It's huge.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.980
And it supports-- like, it's
just running on App Engine,

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:42.770
and it runs very well.

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:45.140
MARK MANDEL: We do something
like 10 times the traffic

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:47.360
of Wikipedia, looking at some
of the slides we've had, like--

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, on App
Engine and in general, yeah.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:49.550
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:50.960
It's pretty impressive.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:52.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's quite
a decent amount of traffic.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:53.190
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:57.720
And for our needs here for
our podcast, I mean, we fit.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:11:58.910
We fit the mold really well.

00:11:58.910 --> 00:11:59.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:11:59.689
Clearly.

00:11:59.689 --> 00:12:02.405
MARK MANDEL: So we can serve
static content through it

00:12:02.405 --> 00:12:03.530
no problem, which is great.

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:06.270
But yeah, we've also
got some Go code

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:08.214
that we're using to
do our analytics.

00:12:08.214 --> 00:12:09.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:11.088
MARK MANDEL: Which is
the other side of things.

00:12:11.088 --> 00:12:13.100
So what we do is
we basically have

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:15.990
just a very simple
redirect in our code

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:18.067
to say where we're
downloading our podcast from.

00:12:18.067 --> 00:12:18.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:21.108
MARK MANDEL: And what that
does is, on that redirect,

00:12:21.108 --> 00:12:22.890
the HTTP request
comes in, we do a 503,

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:26.919
it redirects to--
it redirects to--

00:12:26.919 --> 00:12:28.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cloud Storage?

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:28.940
MARK MANDEL: Cloud
Storage. [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:30.830
my brain just fell over.

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:33.237
Yeah, so it sends off a request
to Google Analytics using

00:12:33.237 --> 00:12:35.320
a little Go library we
have, basically saying hey,

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:36.904
here's an event of
a podcast download,

00:12:36.904 --> 00:12:37.861
and here's the podcast.

00:12:37.861 --> 00:12:38.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:41.520
MARK MANDEL: So we're able to
leverage Google Cloud-- Google

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:44.690
Analytics and use
all its tooling,

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:47.280
and basically be able to
still use all the code on App

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:48.290
Engine at the same time.

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:49.790
So we can have
dynamic code running,

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:52.510
we can have static
content at the same time,

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:53.850
deployment's really easy--

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:54.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:54.940
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
it's pretty sweet.

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:55.715
It's actually really nice.

00:12:55.715 --> 00:12:57.131
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's very nice.

00:12:57.131 --> 00:13:00.070
So for the people that
are curious about it,

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:03.500
what's the library that
you're using for Go Analytics?

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:05.615
MARK MANDEL: It's
Go Ogle Analytics,

00:13:05.615 --> 00:13:06.965
and it'll be in the show notes.

00:13:06.965 --> 00:13:08.506
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's a great name.

00:13:08.506 --> 00:13:09.520
MARK MANDEL: It's a great name.

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:11.186
I think the package
name is actually GA.

00:13:11.186 --> 00:13:12.274
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Ah, OK.

00:13:12.274 --> 00:13:13.940
MARK MANDEL: When it
actually gets used.

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:15.750
It's actually a
really nice library.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:17.564
It uses Google Analytics
measurement API.

00:13:17.564 --> 00:13:18.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:19.590
MARK MANDEL: You
can send any data.

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:21.500
I'm sure we'll get more
sophisticated with it.

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:22.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
I'll give it a try.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:22.610
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:23.710
It's pretty nice.

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:24.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:24.590
OK.

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:27.725
So now we've seen how
to store our data,

00:13:27.725 --> 00:13:31.210
both files like audio
files and everything else,

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:33.008
all the HTML, CSS,
images and so on.

00:13:33.008 --> 00:13:33.716
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:13:33.716 --> 00:13:36.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We've
seen how we manage--

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:37.930
how we measure our success.

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:40.300
But how do we
cause that success?

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:43.652
How do we know how to make
people realize that there's

00:13:43.652 --> 00:13:44.610
a new episode up there?

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:46.026
MARK MANDEL: So
the big thing here

00:13:46.026 --> 00:13:48.030
is what's called,
like, an RSS feed.

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:50.240
I think it stands for
Really Simple Syndication.

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:51.380
And there's a few
other versions.

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:52.296
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Good.

00:13:52.296 --> 00:13:54.344
MARK MANDEL: It's
essentially an XML feed.

00:13:54.344 --> 00:13:56.010
If anyone's been
involved with blogging,

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:58.210
they've probably seen this
in one way or another.

00:13:58.210 --> 00:13:59.970
It's an XML format
that says hey,

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:03.160
I just wrote a new blog post
or a new episode of something.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:05.067
So when podcasting
came around, it sort of

00:14:05.067 --> 00:14:06.150
got extended a little bit.

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:08.717
So not only is it like, hey,
I just put out a blog post,

00:14:08.717 --> 00:14:10.300
it's like, hey, I
put out a blog post,

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:12.750
and it's got this
audio attached to it.

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:14.580
And there are some
extensions for iTunes

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:15.750
and some extensions
for other things

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.470
like that to be able
to put some metadata

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:18.721
around that audio file.

00:14:18.721 --> 00:14:20.970
But it becomes a really
simple way of letting everyone

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:23.370
know who's subscribing,
hey, there's a new podcast,

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:25.722
you might want to listen
to it, it's kind of cool,

00:14:25.722 --> 00:14:26.532
and come on down.

00:14:26.532 --> 00:14:27.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's nice.

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:32.010
And so once we have that RSS
feed that we're publishing,

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:33.570
how will people find it?

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:35.660
Like they can go to
GCPpodcast.com, sure.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:36.410
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:38.260
And there's a link
directly there.

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:39.815
So you could just
go to GCPpodcast.

00:14:39.815 --> 00:14:42.970
If your podcast-- if
you've got a podcast app

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:46.270
or anything like that, you
can just point at the XML feed

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:47.010
quite easily.

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:48.250
It'll be on iTunes.

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:49.900
We'll probably get it on
Stitcher at some point soon.

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:50.325
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:14:50.325 --> 00:14:50.750
Very nice.

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:52.083
MARK MANDEL: I'm sure if-- yeah.

00:14:52.083 --> 00:14:53.814
It should be pretty
easy to get on board.

00:14:53.814 --> 00:14:54.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:58.050
So the last part is,
OK, sure, so now we

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:00.632
have a static content--
a static site.

00:15:00.632 --> 00:15:01.340
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:03.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What if
people want to comment on it?

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:04.606
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:15:04.606 --> 00:15:07.505
So what we decided
to do was basically

00:15:07.505 --> 00:15:09.670
use some existing communities.

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:11.840
So as we said before, if
people want to contact us,

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:14.330
they can do it on Reddit and
they can do it on Google+.

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:16.622
So we're going to put a
post there for every episode

00:15:16.622 --> 00:15:18.580
and just say, hey, if
you want to add comments,

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:19.990
please come talk to us here.

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:20.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:21.300
MARK MANDEL: It makes
life really simple,

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:22.680
and we can take advantage
of those communities.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.590
So I think that's going to
hopefully work quite well.

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:25.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm actually
very excited, especially

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:26.490
about Reddit.

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:27.240
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:27.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
think that Reddit

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:29.597
is a very active community,
and hopefully we're

00:15:29.597 --> 00:15:31.680
going to get nice conversations
going on in there.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:32.950
MARK MANDEL: I haven't
participated that much

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:33.380
in Reddit.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:33.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, really?

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:34.260
MARK MANDEL: I
have to be honest.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:34.970
No.

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:36.860
But yeah, I'm always
open to new things,

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:37.390
so that sounds like fun.

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:37.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I like it.

00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:38.720
I think it's going to be fun.

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:39.386
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:15:39.386 --> 00:15:40.364
Cool.

00:15:40.364 --> 00:15:41.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.840
So I think that, with that,
we kind of finished how

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.650
to run a podcast on the cloud.

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:47.060
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I think so.

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:47.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That was pretty nice.

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:47.610
Cool.

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:48.690
That was pretty easy.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:49.150
MARK MANDEL: That was-- yeah.

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:50.066
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:50.066 --> 00:15:53.682
So we said that we had the
cool thing of the week.

00:15:53.682 --> 00:15:54.390
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:55.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Then
the main content.

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:57.393
